    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Patients exposed to chemotherapy during cancer treatment frequently present with neurocognitive deficits that are debilitating and lasting, commonly known as “chemobrain.” This set of symptoms is anatomically localized to the hippocampus and frontal subcortical white matter, where oligodendrocyte precursor cells (OPCs) are essential to development and maintenance of white matter. Previous studies have shown that these cells are especially vulnerable to chemotherapeutic exposure, suggesting the possibility that OPCs might be a therapeutic target in future treatments for chemotherapy-induced dysfunction. Methotrexate (MTX), an antimetabolite used frequently to treat childhood cancers, has been shown to impair white matter integrity in human patients, but the specific mechanisms of its effects on OPCs have not been elucidated. We show here that there is a significant depletion of the white matter OPC population following chemotherapy administration, while the grey matter OPC population remains intact. This incongruity is not explained by differential survival, as when grey or white matter OPCs are exposed to methotrexate in vitro, neither population exhibits significant cell death at concentrations achieved in the brain following systemic MTX administration. By gaining a better understanding of the cell-intrinsic and microenvironmental mechanisms that govern OPC population dynamics and function, and by creating a faithful mouse model for continued investigation, this research provides compelling evidence for the OPC as a therapeutic target candidate in the treatment of “chemobrain.”",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
